$DIR/main.p:9:35: cannot return any(v), an interface holding a copy of a T in box's frame
    any(v) is an interface that points at a copy of its value in box's frame.
    Converting a value of type parameter T to an interface puts the value in the
    interface itself when the type argument fits in one word, such as a pointer,
    and otherwise puts a copy of the value in the frame of the function that
    converts it (here box, at main.p:9), and the interface points at that copy.
    The program uses box with T = Big, which is larger than one word. That frame
    is reclaimed when box returns, so the interface would point at memory that
    no longer holds the value.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the value as a T, changing the result type to T, and let the caller
    convert it to an interface where it is used:
        return any(v)
    Or call box only with type arguments that fit in one word, such as pointers.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
